{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_83","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"toolshed_web_search","tool":"call_web_search","arguments":{"web_search_instructions":"What is a Stripe key prefix rkcs_test? Is it usable as a server-side API secret for Stripe Checkout Sessions, and what key should Stripe Projects provide for backend API calls? Use official Stripe documentation if possible."},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Research Report: Stripe Key Prefix rkcs_test\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe prefix `rkcs_test` is not recognized in Stripe's official documentation. Stripe API keys typically have prefixes like `sk_test_`, `sk_live_`, `pk_test_`, `pk_live_`, and `rk_test_` for restricted keys. For server-side API calls, including creating Stripe Checkout Sessions, Stripe recommends using secret keys (`sk_...`) or restricted keys (`rk_...`) with appropriate permissions.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n- **Finding 1:** The prefix `rkcs_test` is not documented in Stripe's official API key formats.\n\n- **Finding 2:** Stripe API keys include secret keys (`sk_...`), publishable keys (`pk_...`), and restricted keys (`rk_...`), each serving different purposes.\n\n- **Finding 3:** For server-side API calls, such as creating Stripe Checkout Sessions, Stripe recommends using secret keys (`sk_...`) or restricted keys (`rk_...`) with the necessary permissions.\n\n- **Finding 4:** Restricted keys (`rk_...`) allow for granular permissions, limiting access to specific API resources, enhancing security.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Source Analysis\n\n### Source 1: API keys | Stripe Documentation\n\n- **URL:** https://docs.stripe.com/keys?locale=en-GB\n\n- **Domain:** docs.stripe.com\n\n- **Publication Date:** Date not specified\n\n- **Source Type:** Official Documentation\n\n- **Credibility:** High (Official Stripe documentation)\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThis documentation outlines the different types of Stripe API keys and their intended uses. It emphasizes the importance of securing API keys and provides best practices for managing them. The document also explains how to create, reveal, delete, and rotate API keys through the Stripe Dashboard.\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n\n- Secret keys (`sk_...`) are intended for server-side use and should be kept private.\n\n- Publishable keys (`pk_...`) are for client-side use and can be public.\n\n- Restricted keys (`rk_...`) offer limited permissions for enhanced security.\n\n**Notable Quotes:**\n\n> \"Use restricted API keys instead of secret keys when possible. Restricted keys limit access to only the specific API resources your integration needs, reducing the impact of a compromised key.\"\n\n---\n\n### Source 2: Authentication | Stripe API Reference\n\n- **URL:** https://docs.stripe.com/api/authentication?lang=connect\n\n- **Domain:** docs.stripe.com\n\n- **Publication Date:** Last year\n\n- **Source Type:** Official Documentation\n\n- **Credibility:** High (Official Stripe documentation)\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThis API reference details how Stripe uses API keys for authentication. It describes the different key prefixes and their corresponding environments (test or live). The document also provides guidance on securing API keys and emphasizes the importance of using HTTPS for all API requests.\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n\n- Test mode secret keys have the prefix `sk_test_`.\n\n- Live mode secret keys have the prefix `sk_live_`.\n\n- Restricted keys (`rk_...`) can be used for granular permissions.\n\n**Notable Quotes:**\n\n> \"Your API keys carry many privileges, so be sure to keep them secure! Do not share your secret API keys in publicly accessible areas such as GitHub, client-side code, and so forth.\"\n\n---\n\n### Source 3: Protecting against compromised API keys : Stripe: Help & Support\n\n- **URL:** https://support.stripe.com/questions/protecting-against-compromised-api-keys\n\n- **Domain:** support.stripe.com\n\n- **Publication Date:** Date not specified\n\n- **Source Type:** Support Documentation\n\n- **Credibility:** High (Official Stripe support documentation)\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThis support article provides best practices for protecting Stripe API keys from compromise. It highlights common pitfalls, such as embedding keys in client-side code, and offers recommendations for secure key management. The document also discusses the use of restricted API keys to limit potential damage from a compromised key.\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n\n- Avoid embedding secret keys in client-side code or public repositories.\n\n- Use restricted keys (`rk_...`) to limit access to specific API resources.\n\n- Regularly rotate API keys and monitor their usage.\n\n**Notable Quotes:**\n\n> \"Use restricted API keys (prefix `rk_`) instead of secret keys (prefix `sk_`) wherever possible. RAKs have only the permissions you assign, so a compromised RAK can do far less damage than a compromised secret key.\"\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Reference Analysis\n\n**Consensus Across Sources:**\n\n- All sources agree on the importance of securing API keys and using restricted keys (`rk_...`) to limit access to specific API resources.\n\n- The recommended practice for server-side API calls, including creating Stripe Checkout Sessions, is to use secret keys (`sk_...`) or restricted keys (`rk_...`) with appropriate permissions.\n\n**Conflicting Information:**\n\n- No conflicting information was found among the sources.\n\n**Information Gaps:**\n\n- The specific prefix `rkcs_test` is not documented in the available sources. Further clarification from Stripe's official support channels may be necessary to understand this prefix.\n\n---\n\n## Research Metadata\n\n- **Search Completed:** 2026-08-22T23:22:36Z\n\n- **Sources Analyzed:** 3 sources\n\n- **Total Content Reviewed:** Approximately 1,200 words\n\n- **Search Confidence:** High (Based on the credibility and consistency of the sources) "}],"_meta":{"openai/widgetSessionId":"f6a93303-37cf-4d12-8150-987f63bd6d74"},"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
